  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In December 2020, FNCCI provincial representatives developed proposals for a membership strategy. The newly elected FNCCI Board prioritised membership support and expansion as one of its vision&apos;s pillars and adopted the new membership strategy, following a further review in a rapidly changing, pandemic induced environment. The strategy is built around provision of services and identifying specific areas of activities. It contains an operationalisation plan in terms of setting up a member services centre, business matchmaking, setting up a legal cell, and supporting and reaching out to MSMEs. The plan also provides FNCCI&apos;s contribution to the Economic transformation of 2030 of Nepal.

To extend outreach to MSMEs, FNCCI launched the Enterprise Competitive Assessment (ENCOMPASS) service in 2019. The Federation of Women Entrepreneurs Association (FWEAN) in Nepal led the implementation of this service. In 2020, faced with the pandemic whilst developing a new membership services strategy, FNCCI revised the tool to include an additional indicator on business resilience. FWEAN formulated a sustainability plan to promote ENCOMPASS as a new service and anticipates an increase in membership and income by 2027. 

FWEAN rolled ENCOMPASS to 50 enterprise members in 2 provinces. In February 2020, FWEAN secured cooperation of Global IME bank Nepal through an MOU (signed on 28th Feb&apos;2020) to promote ENCOMPASS among Women Entrepreneurs and enhance access to finance to women entrepreneurs. 

FNCCI&apos;s board adopted a new membership development strategy in August 2021. FNCCI, FWEAN and Federation of Handicrafts Association of Nepal (FHAN) have a new revised ENCOMPASS tool on Competitiveness for its members. This tool reinforces the FNCCI membership services development strategy and constitutes part of its operationalization. In January 2020, the ILO negotiated with the Global IME bank Nepal to more widely promote ENCOMPASS and led the technical discussions which culminated in the signing of a MOU between FWEAN, Global IME bank and ILO Kathmandu on 28th February 2020. The MOU included an understanding that the bank would consider granting special facilities to Women Entrepreneurs having ENCOMPASS certification. 

After the ENCOMPASS tool was revised with an additional indicator on business resilience, the ILO conducted a five day on-line training of trainers (5th to 9th October 2020)  for 21 regional representatives in Kathmandu from FNCCI, FWEAN, and Federation of Handicrafts&apos; Association (FHAN) to promote, assess and certify MSMEs on revised ENCOMPASS tool. The ILO also translated the training tool to Nepali. The training focussed on how to assess MSMEs in terms of the ENCOMPASS Indicators and promotion of ENCOMPASS among their respective members.

After FWEAN conducted the training on ENCOMPASS among its 50 enterprise members, the ILO compiled a collection of success stories related by the FWEAN women entrepreneurs who followed the ENCOMPASS training.

The ILO supported the design of FNCCI&apos;s membership strategy. Additionally, the ITC-ILO and ILO conducted a training on membership development for FNCCI on 7th and 8th December 2020. The training was conducted for the newly appointed members of the FNCCI Executive Committee. The training was intended to create awareness of the importance of having a strategic membership development plan and how to proceed on it. The ILO also provided technical advice on several iterations of the strategy and its implementation modalities. Based on the advice given FNCCI decided to formulate and adopt a Membership services development strategy.</narrative>
   </description>
